WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.100
Neat, secure wire loops, wraps and coils.

2
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:09.000
This is the video for Module 2 of Advanced Beadwork and Jewellery Making: wire work.

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:16.370
Wire turns beads and stones into pendants, chains, connectors and clasps you make yourself.

4
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:24.920
A loop that is off-centre or slightly open is the most common reason handmade jewellery looks cheap or falls apart.

5
00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:30.990
So the goal is simple. Every loop the same size, centred and fully closed.

6
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:38.130
Then coils, spirals and woven wire make customers ask, did you really make this by hand?

7
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:55.740
Two things decide how wire behaves. First, gauge, which means thickness. The numbers run backwards. 18 gauge is about 1.0 mm. 20 gauge is about 0.8 mm. 26 gauge is thin, for coiling.

8
00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:05.990
Second, temper, which means hardness. Dead soft wire is for wrapping and weaving. Half hard holds its shape, for ear wires and clasps.

9
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:12.700
Every bend makes wire harder. Bend the same spot too often and it snaps, so make each bend once.

10
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.830
Step one. Leave about 4 to 5 cm of wire above the bead.

11
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:28.980
Step two. Grip the wire with chain nose pliers about 3 mm above the bead and bend it 90 degrees. That 3 mm gap is where your wraps will sit.

12
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:42.200
Step three. Place round nose pliers at the bend and wrap the wire over the top jaw and down around the bottom jaw to make a loop. Mark a spot on the jaw so every loop is the same size.

13
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.970
Step four. If you are joining to another part, slip it on now.

14
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:54.320
Step five. Hold the loop flat and wrap the tail around the stem 2 or 3 times, coils touching.

15
00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:59.820
Step six. Trim the tail flush and press the end in so nothing sticks out.

16
00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:09.390
Simple loops are quick and can be opened, so they suit earrings. A wrapped loop cannot pull open, so use it for bracelets and necklaces.

17
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:21.490
To open a jump ring, hold it with two chain nose pliers and twist the ends sideways, one hand away from you, one toward you. Reverse the twist to close it with no gap.

18
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.970
Never pull the ends apart into a C shape. It weakens the ring, and it will never close neatly again.

19
00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:39.720
Here is a worked example. Esther in Ibadan is joining 8 mm stone beads with wrapped loops, for a customer whose wrist measures 16 cm.

20
00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:44.980
She adds 2 cm of ease, so the finished length is 18 cm.

21
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:52.440
The clasp, jump ring and end loop take about 2 cm, so the linked section must be 16 cm.

22
00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:59.170
She makes one test link and measures it: 1.6 cm from loop end to loop end.

23
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:14.860
16 divided by 1.6 gives 10 links. She cuts 10 cm of 22 gauge wire per link, and the bracelet measures 18 cm, just as planned. Always measure one test link before you calculate.

24
00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:29.250
Coils use a lot of fine wire, so measure first. For example, Aisha in Kano coiled 50 cm of wire and got 4.5 cm of coil: about 11.1 cm of wire per centimetre.

25
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:37.260
Keep the thin wire at a right angle to the core, and push the coils together every few turns so no gaps show.

26
00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:46.020
For flat spirals, let your thumb do the bending, not the pliers. For earrings, make both spirals before anything else.

27
00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:57.020
In woven wire, the thin wire goes over the top frame wire, down between, around the bottom wire and back up. Count the passes so both sides match.

28
00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:08.390
The common mistake is straightening a kink again and again. It will snap later in the customer's hands. Unwrap back to before the kink and continue with a new piece.

29
00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.260
Before any piece leaves your bench, check it. Wear safety glasses whenever you cut.

30
00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:20.650
Run a stocking over the piece. If it catches, a sharp end is sticking out, so tuck it or file it.

31
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:28.150
Press a wrapped stone from front and back. If it clicks or moves, tighten the frame. And check every loop is closed.

32
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:39.230
Practise on copper first. Wrap a pendant, make 20 practice loops and a linked bracelet, and build a sampler card of coils, spirals and woven wire.

33
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.520
Continue with the lessons below.
